<p>Dude, dude, dude, it's not the quotes that are correct that we're criticising, it's the stuff you've put alongside the quotes that make it unbelievable. It reads like you've just skimmed the manifesto for choice quotes, then made up the rest (and incorrectly at that, unfortunately).</p>
<p>Like I said in chat, I feel like if the article actually played with the inaccuracies, it could be quite fun. But as the article is right now, it's seems like the author didn't understand the source material, not the SCP itself. The comments here saying otherwise just read like ex post facto statements trying to say that the article says stuff that it doesn't right now.</p>
<p>The conversation we had that I showed above pretty much sums it up. To quote from of my favourite critics of historical films, &quot;If you don't give a damn about the source material, why the hell should I care about what you've made?&quot;</p>

